// boxcxx — <iostream>
// The four narrow standard stream objects [iostream.objects], bound to the
// BoxOS Current spine.
//
// This header was absent on purpose for most of the epic's life, and the
// reason it was absent is worth stating: BoxOS has no stdin/stdout/stderr.
// It has no descriptor table to number them in, no shell that inherits them,
// and no parent that decides where they point. What it has is Current — a
// channel named by a TAG and resolved when it is opened. So these objects are
// not a Unix model in C++ clothing; they are the standard's names for three
// BoxOS channels:
//
//   std::cout, std::cerr, std::clog  ->  the "screen" Current
//   std::cin                         ->  the "keyboard" Current
//
// std::print has routed through that same screen channel since the spine
// landed, and these objects share the very handle it uses (__print::Console),
// so `std::cout << a; std::print("{}", b);` emits a then b — one road, one
// order. Mixing them is not merely allowed, it is exact.
//
// ── two honest differences from a hosted implementation ─────────────────
//
// * std::cin NEVER reaches end of file. The keyboard Current is a live
//   console: it blocks for the next line and has no terminator, because a
//   console has no end. There is no Ctrl-D here to make one. Code shaped like
//   `while (std::getline(std::cin, s))` therefore runs forever rather than
//   falling out — which is the truth about the channel, not a defect in the
//   stream. Read a known number of lines, or drive input from a file.
//
// * std::cerr and std::clog go to the SCREEN, not to a separate diagnostic
//   channel. BoxOS does have one — the serial "log" Current, box::current::log
//   — but a std::cerr that only reached a serial cable would be invisible to
//   anyone running on real hardware without one, and silently losing an error
//   message is worse than not distinguishing it. The distinction is available
//   under its own name; it is not smuggled into cerr.
//
// The stream objects are built by a constructor at priority 102 — after the
// TLS bootstrap at 101, and before every default-priority global in the
// program, so a user's own global constructor may write to std::cout.
#ifndef BOXCXX_IOSTREAM
#define BOXCXX_IOSTREAM

#include <ios>
#include <istream>
#include <ostream>
#include <streambuf>

namespace std {

extern istream cin;
extern ostream cout;
extern ostream cerr;
extern ostream clog;

// [iostream.objects]/1 requires that including this header guarantees the
// objects are constructed before use. One of these per translation unit; the
// counter inside it is what flushes the streams on the way out, while they
// are still alive (they outlive it — they are constructed earlier, so they
// are destroyed later).
static ios_base::Init __ioinit;

} // namespace std

#endif // BOXCXX_IOSTREAM
